# ## Study: `dose-response-crossparadigm`
#
# **Question.** Do the two substrates agree on the SIGN of the dose response over initial viral load (more load -> more infection), robustly across seeds?
#
# **Purpose.** More initially-infected cells secrete more virus into the shared field, raising the per-cell infection probability (Pr = 1 - exp(-g_hv * virus)) everywhere, so the final infected count rises with the initial dose on either substrate.
#
# **Claim.** RE-RUN 2026-11-05 on the in-sheet immune vertex substrate (re-calibrated motility): figure regenerated; the STRICT-DIRECTION behavior test (highest dose ends with more infection than the lowest, on both backends) still passes. Earlier reading:   Both backends agree on the DIRECTION of the dose response: a HIGHER initial viral load (more initially-infected epithelial cells) ends with STRICTLY more infection than a LOWER load, on every backend and seed tested. This is a directional (lowest-vs-highest) result; intermediate-dose monotonicity is seed-dependent and is NOT claimed.

